+Domain Commit
+-------------
+
+A HART may need to start in one domain, which sets the system up, and then
+give that domain up for good and continue in a narrower one. The
+experimental domain commit SBI extension (extension ID **0x08004246**,
+enabled by **CONFIG_SBI_ECALL_DCOMMIT**) provides that move:
+
+* **Function 0 (commit)** reads no argument. It succeeds only when the
+  calling HART runs in the non-ROOT domain of least initialization order
+  that lists it, exactly one other non-ROOT domain lists the HART, that
+  domain names the HART as its boot HART, and that domain has an SBI call
+  allowlist (which may be empty). The HART then leaves its domain and
+  enters the other one at its next address and in its next mode, with a0
+  holding the HART id and a1 the next arg1, and the call does not return.
+  Otherwise the call returns **SBI_ERR_DENIED** and changes nothing. No
+  HART can commit back: the domain it enters has the greater
+  initialization order.
+* **Function 1 (digest)** takes a word index in a0 and returns in a1 that
+  word of the configuration digest (**config-digest**), most significant
+  word first: four words on RV64. It returns **SBI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED**
+  when there is no digest, **SBI_ERR_DENIED** to a HART in the ROOT domain
+  or in a domain with an allowlist, and **SBI_ERR_INVALID_PARAM** for an
+  index out of range.
+
+Whenever OpenSBI configures the memory protection of a HART for a domain
+with an SBI call allowlist (at a commit, at a warm boot, or at a resume
+from a non-retentive suspend), it also clears the **menvcfg.CBIE** field
+of the HART, so that cache-block invalidation is illegal below M-mode, and
+reads every PMP entry back to check it against the regions of the domain.
+A mismatch, or a domain switch that cannot program the PMP, stops the HART
+in M-mode.
+
+Each HART keeps a commit record in the data of OpenSBI, written only by
+that HART: whether it is committed, how many access faults were handed
+back to it, how many SBI calls its allowlist refused, how many allowed
+calls returned an error, and, once committed, its **menvcfg**, **mseccfg**
+and first 16 PMP entries. When the device tree holds a configuration
+digest, OpenSBI prints the digest at boot and every record just before a
+system reset.
+
+The per-HART contexts of domain switches are allocated once at boot, for
+every HART in every domain that lists it, so a switch never allocates.
 
 Domain Device Tree Bindings
 ---------------------------
@@ -130,6 +180,11 @@ The DT properties of a domain configuration DT node are as follows:
 
 * **compatible** (Mandatory) - The compatible string of the domain
   configuration. This DT property should have value *"opensbi,domain,config"*
+* **config-digest** (Optional) - A 256 bit digest of the configuration that
+  the device tree describes, as eight 32 bit cells, most significant first.
+  OpenSBI does not interpret it: it prints it at boot and serves it through
+  the domain commit extension. A length other than eight cells stops the
+  boot.
 
 ### Domain Memory Region Node
 
@@ -205,6 +260,14 @@ The DT properties of a domain instance DT node are as follows:
   whether the domain instance is allowed to do system reset.
 * **system-suspend-allowed** (Optional) - A boolean flag representing
   whether the domain instance is allowed to do system suspend.
+* **ecall-allowlist** (Optional) - The SBI calls that the HARTs of the
+  domain instance may make. Each entry is four 32 bit cells: an extension
+  ID, a function ID, an argument index and a maximum. The argument index
+  is 0 to 5 for the a0 to a5 registers, in which case a call matches the
+  entry only while that register holds at most the maximum, or 0xffffffff
+  when no argument is checked. At most eight entries are allowed. An empty
+  property allows no SBI call; without the property the SBI calls of the
+  domain instance are not filtered. A malformed property stops the boot.
 
